cabal-version:   3.0
name:            agentic
version:         0.1.0.0
synopsis:        A Haskell port of agent-cat's raw syntax, guards and string layer
description:
  Week one of the port of the Lean 4 formalization at agent-cat: the @Raw@
  AST and its JSON codec, the five term-level guards, the two ask counts, and
  the string layer. Conformance is decided by the frozen corpus at
  @agent-cat\/test\/corpus@, replayed by the @tier0@ executable.
  .
  Week two adds the semantic core: the typed @Plan@ and its static folds
  (@Agentic.Plan@), its meaning in a world — traces and bills — (@Agentic.World@),
  and the production surface whose combinators elaborate to the same @Plan@ the
  Lean checker elaborates the corresponding surface construct to
  (@Agentic.Builder@). The @tier1@ executable rebuilds curated corpus cases in
  that surface and holds both the printed program and the whole reply against
  the frozen entries.
  .
  Weeks three and four add the live bisimulation: property generators over the
  builder and over raw syntax (@Agentic.Gen@), the reply assembly both runners
  share (@Agentic.Observe@), a line-delimited JSON client for the Lean oracle
  subprocess (@Agentic.Oracle@), and the @bisim@ executable, which draws
  programs, worlds and string-layer inputs and holds each one against a live
  @conformance-oracle@ rather than against a frozen file.
  .
  Week five makes the library runnable: an @IO@ interpreter with the Lean
  runner's memo discipline and its decode\/re-ask loop (@Agentic.Exec@), an
  adapter that puts every question — model, tool and person alike — to one live
  @agent-deck@ session (@Agentic.AgentDeck@), the flagship and the smallest
  program rebuilt in the builder as a shared internal library (@Example.Harden@,
  the @examples@ library), and the @agentic-run@ executable, which plans, prices
  and runs them.
  .
  Week six replaces what a human /writes/, and nothing under it: @Agentic.WF@'s
  @[wf|…|]@ prompt quoter and @Agentic.Workflow@'s indexed block — whose one
  @do@ grammar is @Agentic.Workflow.Do@ — are sugar whose every combinator is
  an application of an @Agentic.Builder@ entry point, so a program written in
  them prints and elaborates through the one pairing tier1 already pins. A
  program is ordinary Haskell: no splice, no bracket, no label. Since a library
  cannot read a Haskell binder's spelling, the surface carries no names at the
  type level and generates the name each binding prints from its depth, which
  an author may override with @named@; a @{hole}@ prints the name its handle
  carries, so binder and hole agree by construction. @Example.Harden@ is
  written that way, and is the proof: the two frozen entries @example-000@ and
  @example-001@, statement for statement, their printed programs pinned up to
  alpha.
  .
  Week seven adds the second transport: @Agentic.Acp@ starts an Agent Client
  Protocol adapter of its own — Claude's or Codex's, or the deterministic
  double at @agent-cat\/test\/stub_adapter.py@ — and speaks line-delimited
  JSON-RPC 2.0 to it over a pipe this process owns. It is the transport that can check what the
  @agent-deck@ one cannot: a prompt is answered with a stop reason, so an act
  whose turn did not complete abandons the run rather than recording a receipt
  for something that did not happen. @agentic-run run EXAMPLE --engine acp@ is
  the surface, and @ci\/acp.sh@ is the gate — against the stub adapter, and
  never against a real agent.
  .
  Week eight gives the authoring surface the two things the production one
  always had and it did not: a @function@ — a name, a parameter list and a
  straight-line body — with @call@, @call_@ and @defining@'s table, and a
  program's @input@s, which are @define@s supplied at the command line rather
  than answers asked of a tool. Neither reaches below @Agentic.Workflow@:
  @Agentic.Builder@ is byte-identical, and a call costs exactly what writing
  the callee's statements at the call site costs. @Example.Isaac@'s
  @review-lite@ is the worked case — its duplicated closing act is one function
  both arms of the router call, and the commit it reviews arrives as
  @agentic-run … --input@.

build-type:      Simple
license:         MIT
author:          John Wiegley
maintainer:      jwiegley@positron.ai

common settings
  ghc-options:      -Wall
  default-language: Haskell2010

  -- The full agreed dependency list, on every target. Several implementers are
  -- writing the modules independently; a shared list means no one's module
  -- fails to build over a missing `build-depends` entry someone else owns.
  --
  -- `QuickCheck` and `process` arrive with week three: the first for
  -- `Agentic.Gen`'s generators, the second for `Agentic.Oracle`'s subprocess
  -- pipes. Both are library dependencies rather than executable-only ones,
  -- because both modules live in the library — the generators and the oracle
  -- client are the conformance surface, and `bisim` is only their driver.
  build-depends:
    , aeson
    , base
    , bytestring
    , containers
    , directory
    , filepath
    , process
    , QuickCheck
    , scientific
    , template-haskell
    , text
    , vector

library
  import:          settings
  hs-source-dirs:  src
  exposed-modules:
    Agentic.Acp
    Agentic.AgentDeck
    Agentic.Builder
    Agentic.Chains
    Agentic.Exec
    Agentic.Gen
    Agentic.Guards
    Agentic.Observe
    Agentic.Oracle
    Agentic.Plan
    Agentic.Raw
    Agentic.Shell
    Agentic.Text
    Agentic.WF
    Agentic.Workflow
    Agentic.Workflow.Do
    Agentic.World

-- The worked examples, written in the authoring surface — `Agentic.Workflow`,
-- which is sugar over the production one: the flagship (corpus entry
-- `example-000`) and the smallest program (`example-001`).
--
-- An internal library rather than a directory shared by two `other-modules`
-- lists, because two components are meant to see the same `Example.Harden` and
-- not two separately compiled copies of it: `tier1` pins the rebuilt flagship
-- against the frozen corpus entry, and `agentic-run` runs the very program
-- that was pinned. Compiling it twice would let the two drift under a
-- conditional or a flag and read as agreement anyway.
--
-- The module is `Example.Harden` and cabal resolves a module name to a path, so
-- the file is `example/Example/Harden.hs` — not `example/Harden.hs`, which
-- builds here on a case-insensitive filesystem and on no Linux at all.
library examples
  import:          settings
  hs-source-dirs:  example
  exposed-modules:
    Example.Harden
    Example.Isaac

  build-depends:   agentic

executable tier0
  import:          settings
  hs-source-dirs:  tier0
  main-is:         Main.hs
  build-depends:   agentic

executable tier1
  import:          settings
  hs-source-dirs:  tier1
  main-is:         Main.hs

  -- `CallVectors` is tier1's alone, and deliberately not in the `examples`
  -- internal library: the three programs in it are conformance fixtures for
  -- the authoring surface's `function`/`call`, not things anybody would run,
  -- and a module `agentic-run` cannot see is a module its example registry
  -- cannot grow a row for.
  other-modules:
    CallVectors
    Cases

  build-depends:
    , agentic
    , agentic:examples

-- The policy gate (test/PolicyProbe.hs): the seven D6/D7 probes the wave-one
-- audit ran by hand, plus four surface refusals, pinned as a program.
-- `lake`-free and corpus-free; run by ci/policies.sh.
--
-- `SurfaceRefusals` is its own module because the four programs in it are
-- written in the authoring surface, and `RebindableSyntax` — which is what an
-- authoring module is — is module-wide: a probe harness that enabled it would
-- find its own `if` reaching `Agentic.Workflow.ifThenElse`.
executable policy-probe
  import:          settings
  hs-source-dirs:  test
  main-is:         PolicyProbe.hs
  other-modules:   SurfaceRefusals
  build-depends:
    , agentic
    , agentic:examples

-- The runnable surface: plan an example, price it, or run it — against the
-- scripted transport, against a live agent-deck session, or against an ACP
-- adapter it starts itself (`--engine acp`).
executable agentic-run
  import:          settings
  hs-source-dirs:  run
  main-is:         Main.hs
  build-depends:
    , agentic
    , agentic:examples

  -- Same reason `bisim` is threaded, one layer out. `Agentic.AgentDeck` bounds
  -- every turn with a wall clock and drains a subprocess's stderr on a second
  -- thread; a wedged session must produce a named failure rather than a hung
  -- run. `Agentic.Acp` needs it for the sharper version of the same claim: its
  -- turn budget is `System.Timeout.timeout` around a request that is blocked
  -- reading the adapter's pipe, and a read is interruptible by an asynchronous
  -- exception only under the threaded runtime.
  ghc-options:     -threaded

executable bisim
  import:          settings
  hs-source-dirs:  bisim
  main-is:         Main.hs
  build-depends:   agentic

  -- The same departure from tier0 and tier1 that `agentic-run` makes, for the
  -- same reason one layer down. `Agentic.Oracle` guards every read from the
  -- subprocess with `System.Timeout.timeout`, and a wedged oracle must produce
  -- a named failure rather than a hung run; the threaded runtime is what makes
  -- that interruption reliable rather than something that happens to work on
  -- this pipe on this platform.
  ghc-options:     -threaded
